# Identity-bound artifacts must reach the working tree byte-for-byte.
#
# Several reference corpora are validated against a SHA-256 recorded in a sibling
# *.identity.json. Git's line-ending conversion changes those bytes: with the
# Git-for-Windows default (core.autocrlf=true) a checkout rewrites LF to CRLF, the
# hash no longer matches, and the loader refuses the file. For
# f4_221_pdb_publics.txt that surfaces as
#
#   ValueError: F4 221 PDB-public dump changed after binding
#
# raised from pdb_publics_f4_221.py::_validate_identity, which aborts
# generate_scripts() with a traceback and takes the whole run down -- so the 38k-record
# 1.11.221 public-symbol corpus is unusable on a default Windows clone no matter which
# executables are staged. The file is bound to its LF form (the bytes stored in the
# index), so -text makes the checkout match the binding on every platform.
scripts/commonlibf4/refs/f4_221_pdb_publics.txt -text

# NOTE for maintainers: bytesig_ported_221.csv and bytesig_ported_og.csv are the
# mirror image -- their recorded hashes match the CRLF *working tree* rather than the
# LF index, so they validate on Windows today and would fail on a checkout that does
# not convert (Linux/macOS, or core.autocrlf=false). Marking them -text as well is the
# consistent fix, but it requires re-recording their artifact_sha256 against the LF
# bytes, so it is deliberately left out of this change.
